-- Passage rg11-connections-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1097d629-e255-5598-b986-4dde68f333d1', 'rg11-connections-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Box That Changed a Harbor$q$, $q$For most of its history, the fictional Port of Calloway ran on muscle and patience. When a cargo ship arrived, crews of dockworkers unloaded it one sack, barrel and crate at a time, lifting goods onto carts and then into the warehouses that lined the waterfront. A large ship could sit at the pier for a week or more, and the narrow harbor streets were crowded with wagons, and later trucks, waiting for their turn.

In 1961, a freight manager named Ilse Varga proposed a different approach. Instead of handling each item separately, shippers would pack goods into steel boxes of a single standard size at the factory. The sealed box would ride a truck to the port, be lifted onto a ship by crane, and be lifted off again at the other end without ever being opened. Varga argued that the time saved at the pier would matter more than any savings at sea.

The port's board approved a trial, but the first results were disappointing. The old piers were too weak to hold the heavy cranes, and many ships could not stack the boxes safely. Calloway spent the next six years rebuilding: it filled in part of the old waterfront, poured new concrete wharves, and installed cranes that could move a loaded box in under two minutes.

Once the new system was running, the changes spread far beyond the docks. A ship that once needed a week in port could now leave in a day, so shipping companies began sending more vessels to Calloway. Because goods stayed sealed, losses from damage and theft fell sharply. The waterfront warehouses, no longer needed, stood empty for a decade before the city turned several of them into apartments and a market hall.

The work of the dockworkers changed most of all. Far fewer workers were needed to move far more cargo, and the dockworkers' union negotiated a retraining program that taught many members to operate the cranes and the new tracking systems. Today Calloway handles twenty times the cargo it did in 1960 with a fraction of the workforce, and the empty warehouses that once marked its decline have become one of the busiest neighborhoods in the city.$q$, 'draft')
