-- Passage rg11-connections-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5e1f05ff-2f43-5931-b1ba-93816a7e3c3c', 'rg11-connections-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$A Clearer Lake, a Different Lake$q$, $q$Lake Oriel, a long glacial lake in the fictional Durnholm district, was for decades known for its green, cloudy water. Each summer, tiny floating algae bloomed near the surface, and swimmers could rarely see their own feet. Then, beginning around 2004, the water changed in a way no one had planned.

The change began with the arrival of the striped pea mussel, a small shellfish that most likely reached the lake attached to the hull of a visiting boat. Each mussel filters several liters of water a day, straining out algae and other floating particles to eat. Because the mussels reproduced quickly and had no local predators, their numbers grew into the billions within five years, and together they filtered the entire volume of the lake every few weeks.

The first effect delighted many residents. By 2010, visitors could see the lake bottom at a depth of six meters, twice as far as before. Yet researchers at the Durnholm Freshwater Station soon noticed that clearer water was not the same as healthier water. With fewer algae drifting near the surface, sunlight reached much deeper, and rooted water plants spread across shallow bays where they had never grown before. Boaters began to complain that thick weeds tangled their propellers.

The fish population shifted as well. Open-water fish that fed on the tiny animals that graze on algae declined, since their food supply had shrunk. Meanwhile, fish that hide and hunt among plants, such as the lake's native pike, became more common in the newly weedy bays. Anglers who had fished the same spots for years found that their usual catch had moved or vanished.

By 2018, the station's lead scientist, Dr. Tomasz Ellery, was describing Lake Oriel as a different lake rather than a cleaner one. His team now counts the mussels each season, and the district requires that boats be washed before they enter any of its lakes. Removing the mussels entirely is not possible, Ellery's reports explain, so the goal has shifted to keeping the same chain of changes from starting in the lakes nearby.$q$, 'draft')
