-- Passage rg11-connections-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('7bcd10dd-2dd1-52ac-9b4b-443eefb87944', 'rg11-connections-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Two Lives of the Fennick Canal$q$, $q$When the fictional Fennick Canal opened in 1832, it was hailed as the fastest route between the coal mines of the upper valley and the mill towns along the coast. Horses walking on a towpath pulled long, narrow boats through a chain of forty-one locks, and a load of coal that had once taken a week to haul by wagon over muddy roads could now reach the coast in three days. Villages along the route grew quickly, and inns, stables and boatyards appeared at nearly every lock.

The canal's success, however, depended on there being no faster alternative. In 1858 a railway opened along the same valley, and trains could carry coal to the coast in a single afternoon. The canal company cut its tolls twice to compete, but each cut reduced the money available for repairs. As maintenance slipped, leaks and silted channels slowed the boats even further, which drove still more customers to the railway.

By 1900 only a handful of boats used the canal, and in 1931 the company closed it. Over the following decades, sections of the channel filled with mud and reeds, several locks collapsed, and parts of the towpath disappeared under farmland. The canal villages shrank as the inns and boatyards that had depended on canal traffic closed.

The canal's second life began with a group of volunteers in 1972. Led by a retired surveyor named Agnieszka Ferreira-Holt, they began clearing a two-kilometer stretch near the village of Brannock on weekends, at first simply to create a pleasant walking path. The cleared stretch attracted walkers, anglers and families, and its popularity convinced the regional council to fund the repair of three locks so that small pleasure boats could use it.

Over the next thirty years, restoration moved lock by lock up the valley. Today the full canal is open again, though it carries tourists rather than coal. Several villages that declined after 1931 have grown once more, this time around boat rentals, cafes and guesthouses. The towpath that horses once walked now forms part of a long-distance hiking trail.$q$, 'draft')
