   $q$Which statement best explains the relationship between the railway and the canal's decline?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The railway and the canal shared customers evenly until a new law forced the canal to close in 1931.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The railway closed the canal directly in 1858, ending traffic on the same day that its first trains ran.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The railway played little part, since the decline was caused entirely by poor maintenance from the start.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-emphasis"},{"text":"The railway started the decline, but the company's toll cuts sped it up by starving the canal of repairs.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The railway started the decline, but the company's toll cuts sped it up by starving the canal of repairs.$q$, $q$Paragraph 2 says the railway offered a faster alternative, and the company's response made things worse because toll cuts reduced repair money. The canal kept running until 1931, so the railway did not close it in 1858.$q$, 'draft', '7bcd10dd-2dd1-52ac-9b4b-443eefb87944')
