insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('fc9c1c42-3621-5dad-8b7b-04e52ba63346', 'rg11-connections-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$A Network on the Ridgetops$q$, $q$In 2012, most households in the fictional hill county of Corran still connected to the internet through old telephone lines, at speeds too slow to stream a lesson video or upload a large file. Telecommunications companies had studied the county several times and concluded that laying new cable across its steep, thinly populated valleys would cost far more than residents could ever repay in monthly fees.

The turning point came from an unlikely place. Hamid Oyelaran-Brooks, a high school physics teacher, noticed that his students could not finish online assignments at home, and he began reading about fixed wireless networks. In such a network, an antenna on a tall structure sends a signal through the air to small receivers mounted on homes within sight of it. Because no cable has to be buried, the cost of reaching a distant farmhouse is far lower.

Oyelaran-Brooks presented the idea at a county meeting, and residents formed a cooperative, a business owned by its members, to build the network. Each member household paid a joining fee, and volunteers with farming and construction experience installed the equipment. The first antenna went up on a grain silo in 2014 and served sixty homes.

The county's geography shaped every decision that followed. Because the signal needed a clear line of sight, the hills that had made cable too expensive now became assets, since an antenna on a ridge could reach several valleys at once. The cooperative negotiated with landowners and the county to place antennas on ridgetops and water towers, and it gradually linked the sites into a chain that passed the signal from one ridge to the next.

Faster connections soon changed daily life in ways members had not fully expected. Students could complete coursework at home, and several residents who had moved away for work returned because they could now do their jobs remotely. Their return added new members to the cooperative, and the extra fees paid for more antennas. By 2022 the network served more than four thousand homes, and the companies that had once dismissed Corran began offering to buy it. The members voted to keep it.$q$, 'draft')
